D={
"Ashoka's edicts":"From about 260 BCE Ashoka had his messages carved on rocks and polished pillars across his empire, mostly in Prakrit in Brahmi script, but in Greek and Aramaic in Afghanistan. They speak of his remorse after Kalinga, tolerance for all sects, medical care for people and animals, and officials (dhamma-mahamattas) to spread welfare. James Prinsep deciphered Brahmi in 1837, recovering Ashoka for history.",
"Great Wall unified by Qin":"After 221 BCE, Qin Shi Huang sent General Meng Tian with 300,000 men to drive back the Xiongnu and link older state walls of rammed earth into a frontier wall. Many forced labourers died. The famous brick-and-stone Great Wall seen today was mostly built by the Ming dynasty.",
"Silk Road opened (Zhang Qian)":"Han Emperor Wu sent Zhang Qian west in 138 BCE to find allies against the Xiongnu; captured for ten years, he escaped and reached Bactria. His reports opened official contact with Central Asia. Silk, horses, paper, Buddhism, spices and ideas soon flowed along routes linking China, India, Persia and Rome.",
"Spartacus revolt":"In 73 BCE the Thracian gladiator Spartacus escaped from Capua; up to 120,000 slaves joined him and defeated several Roman armies. Crassus crushed them in 71 BCE and crucified 6,000 along the Appian Way.",
"Battle of Actium":"On 2 September 31 BCE Octavian's fleet under Agrippa defeated Antony and Cleopatra off western Greece. Both later took their lives, and Octavian became Augustus, sole master of Rome.",
"Teutoburg Forest":"In 9 CE the Germanic leader Arminius ambushed three Roman legions under Varus, destroying them. Rome gave up conquering Germany beyond the Rhine.",
"Kanishka's council":"Kanishka convened the Fourth Buddhist Council in Kashmir (about 1st–2nd century CE), compiling commentaries and supporting the rise of Mahayana Buddhism.",
"Yellow Turban rebellion":"In 184 CE peasants following the Daoist Zhang Jue rose against Han corruption and famine, wearing yellow headbands. Warlords who crushed it divided China, ending the Han.",
"Crisis of the Third Century":"Between 235 and 284 CE Rome had over 20 emperors, invasions, plague and inflation; the empire split into three before Aurelian and Diocletian restored it.",
"Constantine legalises Christianity":"The Edict of Milan (313) granted religious freedom; Constantine favoured Christianity, and Theodosius made it the state religion (380).",
"Sack of Rome by Visigoths":"In 410 Alaric's Visigoths sacked Rome, the first time in 800 years; Augustine wrote City of God in response.",
"Huna invasions of India":"Hunas (Hephthalites) under Toramana and Mihirakula invaded north India from about 480, weakening the Guptas and destroying Buddhist monasteries.",
"Battle of Badr":"In 624 Muhammad's 313 followers defeated a larger Meccan force, strengthening the Muslim community.",
"Battle of Qadisiyyah":"In 636 Arab Muslim forces defeated the Sasanian army in Iraq, opening Persia to conquest.",
"Xuanzang's journey to India":"The monk Xuanzang travelled overland from 629 to 645, studied at Nalanda, and brought 657 texts to China; his record is a key source.",
"Tang–Tibet wars":"The Tibetan Empire fought Tang China for control of the Tarim basin and even occupied Chang'an briefly in 763.",
"An Lushan rebellion":"In 755 the general An Lushan rebelled; the eight-year war devastated the Tang and weakened central control.",
"Charlemagne crowned emperor":"On Christmas Day 800, Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne emperor in Rome, reviving the idea of a western empire.",
"Viking raid on Lindisfarne":"On 8 June 793 Vikings sacked the monastery of Lindisfarne, beginning the Viking Age.",
"Battle of Manzikert":"In 1071 the Seljuk Alp Arslan defeated and captured Byzantine Emperor Romanos IV, opening Anatolia to the Turks.",
"Mahmud of Ghazni raids Somnath":"In 1025 Mahmud of Ghazni raided the Somnath temple in Gujarat, one of 17 raids into India.",
"Angkor Wat built":"Suryavarman II built Angkor Wat in the early 12th century as a Vishnu temple.",
"Saladin retakes Jerusalem":"After defeating the crusaders at Hattin, Saladin took Jerusalem in 1187 without a massacre.",
"Fourth Crusade sacks Constantinople":"In 1204 crusaders sacked Christian Constantinople.",
"Battle of Ain Jalut":"In 1260 the Mamluks under Qutuz and Baybars defeated the Mongols in Palestine.",
"Marco Polo in China":"Marco Polo served Kublai Khan from about 1275 to 1292; his book inspired Europe.",
"Mongol invasions of Japan":"Kublai sent fleets in 1274 and 1281; typhoons ('kamikaze') wrecked them.",
"Great Famine of Europe":"From 1315 to 1317 rains caused crop failures; perhaps 10% of northern Europeans died.",
"Hundred Years' War":"England and France fought from 1337 to 1453; Joan of Arc inspired French victory.",
"Timur sacks Delhi":"In December 1398 Timur sacked Delhi, killing tens of thousands.",
"Zheng He's voyages":"From 1405 to 1433 Ming admiral Zheng He led seven fleets to Asia and Africa.",
"Battle of Grunwald":"In 1410 Poland–Lithuania defeated the Teutonic Knights.",
"Fall of Granada and Reconquista":"In January 1492 Granada surrendered, ending Muslim rule in Spain; Jews were expelled.",
"Portuguese take Goa":"Afonso de Albuquerque took Goa from Bijapur in 1510.",
"Protestant Reformation":"Martin Luther's 95 Theses (1517) began the Reformation.",
"Battle of Khanwa":"In 1527 Babur defeated Rana Sanga's Rajput confederacy.",
"Second Battle of Panipat":"In 1556 Akbar's forces defeated Hemu.",
"Battle of Haldighati":"In 1576 Maharana Pratap fought Akbar's army under Man Singh.",
"Spanish Armada defeated":"In 1588 England defeated Spain's Armada.",
"Imjin War":"From 1592 to 1598 Japan invaded Korea; Admiral Yi defeated their fleets.",
"Battle of Sekigahara":"In 1600 Tokugawa Ieyasu won, founding the shogunate.",
}
